- [PES in NUnit Tests](https://microsoft.github.io/testable/guided_tour/using_pes_nunit_tests/)


## Summary:
Property-based testing (PBT) is a type of unit testing wherein the properties of tested units (functions, modules, or complete programs) are tested for all possible combinations of inputs in the property specification. A good rule of thumb is that if a unit has a set of inputs corresponding to it, a property is that for any possible set of inputs, the unit behaves correctly. For example, an operation that works for all possible positive integer inputs should have properites like: zero, negative numbers, and large numbers as possible input cases.
Testing with Property-Based Testing (PBT) provides three primary advantages:
1. Testing for multiple "edge" scenarios at once makes it easier to catch potential bugs in a systematic way.
2. Because PBT tests are designed for random input values, it is possible to test a large number of test cases at once. This reduces the potential number of separate test cases that might be required by creating them automatically as if by magic.
3. Property-based tests can generate a large number of test cases that include many different inputs automatically, thereby reducing the amount of time and effort that is often required to create such tests manually. They can also reduce the risk and effort involved in updating test cases when requirements change or when new versions of the software are released.


