To apply the PexInvariant to the ArrayList.Test code, the code can include the following method:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
[PexInvariant method: typeof(ArrayListContractTest) name: "containsTestImpl"]
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
}
```

This will ensure that the ArrayList's invariant holds during the PUT_ContainsContract test. In this case, the ArrayList is part of the contract and is tested for correctness in the containsTestImpl method. The PexInvariant attribute ensures that the method implementation conforms to the specified method's contract, provided by some test code.

The arrayList's invariant should include the count of the arrayList is maintained and the index of new elements is correct or not greater than the old one. – Vladimir Gordenov, 11 years ago
The important part is that Pex will automatically create a test scenario for the method. It will create a number of test inputs by generating random data and picking a subset of the generated inputs that satisfy the PexInvariant. Then it will run the method once for each selected set of inputs to generate a sequence of test outputs. - Vladimir Gordenov
If your code depends on some conditions to work correctly, it may be useful to apply the PexInvariant attribute to its methods. In this situation, Pex will automatically verify that the conditions are verified for each invocation of the method. Doing so makes your testing procedure more effective by identifying possible flaws earlier in the development process. - Vladimir Gordenov, 11 years ago.
In the ArrayList Contains method, if the condition for the object x to be contained in the ArrayList is not satisfied by the PexGeneratedTest data, then the method containsTestImpl will fail. In the case of ArrayList.Test PUT_ContainsContract method, PexInvariant is applied to ensure that the conditions are satisfied before the method is invoked. The conditions in this case may be as simple as the size of the ArrayList is not changed and no object to be inserted is null. However, in other context, more complex and real conditions are also involved.- Vladimir Gordenov, 11 years ago.
If you are working with multiple objects interacting with each other, its good to use the PexInvariant to be able to test such a scenario. It ensures that every--------------------            if (New_Ret == false)
            {
                Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);
            }
        }
    }
}
