原文链接：https://auth0.com/blog/testing-csharp-with-pester/原文标题：Testing C# with Pester Part 3 — PInvoke Contract Testing原文作者：Moe Jiang原文日期：Jan 16, 2021原文出处：Auth0 Blog---

译者：刘宇航

译文类型：原创

原文内容总结：本文将向读者介绍如何使用Pester进行C#的PInvoke contract testing，重点介绍了ArrayList类，并对其进行测试，以确保其符合预期的行为和处理不同边界情况的能力。通过PInvoke contract testing，可以有效地发现类的方法可能存在的问题，并确保代码的稳定性。

译文内容：在编写代码时，全面的测试可以帮助我们发现并修复潜在的问题，确保代码的正确性。在本部分，我将向您介绍如何使用Pester库进行C#代码的PInvoke contract testing。

PInvoke contract testing是通过对类的相关方法进行测试，以确保其符合预期的行为和处理不同边界情况的能力。在对ArrayList类进行PInvoke contract testing时，我们测试了`Contains()`方法。

通过对ArrayList的测试，我们发现了几个边界情况。例如，当测试`Contains()`方法时，ArrayList中包含重复元素的情况和ArrayList的大小为0的情况都被测试到了。如果ArrayList中包含重复元素，`Contains()`方法应该返回`true`，如果ArrayList的大小为0，`Contains()`方法--------------------            int PrevIndex = Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 ? Old_arrListIndexOfX : 0;
            int LastIndex = Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 ? Old_arrListLastIndexOfX : 0;

            if (!New_Ret) {
                Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= PrevIndex);
                Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= LastIndex);
                Debug.Assert((PrevIndex == 0 && LastIndex == 0) || (PrevIndex == LastIndex));             
            }
        } 
    }
}
```

When the `arrList.Contains(x)` returns `false`, then the index of `x` before calling the method is greater than or equal to the index of `x` after calling the method. 

It is a good practice to thoroughly test each public method in your application. In this case, you should check each public method and make sure that it behaves as expected. You can use pex methods and parameters to achieve that and to ensure that your code behaves as per specification.

Also, it's important to note that the PexAssert.Range method might not be the best choice always because it can provide false positive results due to the nature of floating point handling. PexAssume.Absurd might be a good choice if a method should only fail under certain assumptions that you have already proved to be true.

It's also a good idea to refine your test coverage: consider what sorts of inputs you will be testing. If you're only testing simple, non-critical methods, perhaps you can focus on verifying the critical path of your application and do some heavier testing in an unsupported context, like a database, web service, or UI elements, if applicable.

For more advanced testing, you may also use third-party tools: tools for finding bugs, quality assurance tools, performance testing tools, etc.

Keep testing, and eventually, you'll find that almost all of your problems have already been solved along the way. Don't get discouraged if some of your tests initially fail, that's perfectly normal and is often a sign that your tests are not yet adequate. Keep refining them, and soon you will find that you have a well-tested application. Happy testing!!!

### PexSettings 

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;

[ExcludeFromCodeCoverage]
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Method | AttributeTargets.Constructor, AllowMultiple = false, Inherited = false)]
public sealed class ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute : Attribute
{
}
```

[PexAllowedException] is an attribute that can be placed onto PexMethods that declare that they should be allowed to throw certain exceptions. These exceptions are normally masked by Pex, so the test runner won't "see" them. This means that the test will fail if a method throws any of these exceptions that you did not specifically allow in your PexMethod attribute.

- Exceptions allowed: ArgumentException, InvalidOperationException, NullReferenceException, etc.

Here is an example of how to use it:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
[PexAllowedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
public void SomeMethodName([PexAssumeNotNull] string x)
{
   //...
}
```

This test will not be stopped if `SomeMethodName` will throw `ArgumentException`, but if it throws other exceptions, the test will fail.


  
































































































































































































































--------------------        }
    }
}
```

> **警告**
对于ArrayList或其他任何集合，由于它在索引与插入顺序中保留插入顺序，所以测试索引时需要小心处理。

### 使用LINQPad实现ArrayList的合同测试

对于LINQPad，你需要安装LINQ库以供使用LINQ查询和操作集合，包括`IEnumerable`和`ArrayList`类型。

以下是使用LINQPad进行LINQ to Objects ArrayList的Contract Test的示例代码：

```csharp
void Main()
{
    ArrayList al = new ArrayList(new int[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 });
    int value = 3;
    bool containsBefore = al.OfType<int>().Contains(value);
    al.Add(value);
    bool containsAfter = al.OfType<int>().Contains(value);

    Console.WriteLine("Value: " + value);
    Console.WriteLine("Contains before: " + containsBefore);
    Console.WriteLine("Contains after: " + containsAfter);
}
```

在合同测试中，我们首先在创建的ArrayList内添加一个值，然后检查值是否在集合中存在，并在不同的时间点进行检查，以查看测试是否有效。

可以看出，这个简单的LINQPad脚本应该能证明ArrayList是否符合合同要求，因为当我们在ArrayList中添加特定的值时，应该返回true，表明它存在于集合中。如果ArrayList不符合合同要求，那么无论我们添加什么值到ArrayList中，"ContainsAfter"总是应该返回true，这将指出来。

这只是一个非常基本的测试，并且仅针对此特定操作-添加值到集合中进行检查。根据Linq的ContractTest框架，你应该编写更多的测试来确保你的ArrayList（或任何其他ArrayList模拟的集合）的质量。

请记住，根据你的ArrayList模拟对象的不同，你的合同测试可能需要更复杂一些。

请注意使用集合不应该为空，也不应该包含重复元素，因为Linq的Contains（）方法只返回true，如果ArrayList或HashSet中存在value。

这绝对不是一个详尽无遗的测试用例集，但希望这能给你一些启示。

