        }

What we have is a property method for the ArrayList that checks if the ArrayList contains a certain value.
Now what we want to test is whether if we call this method for an element that exists in the ArrayList, its index (IndexOf) and LastIndexOf values will not change. If that element doesn't exist in the ArrayList, its index (IndexOf) and LastIndexOf values will decrease.
This is a contract test which checks if the preconditions (count before and after the operation, the index of the element before the operation, the last index of the element before the operation) are satisfied by the postconditions (count after the operation, the index of the element after the operation, the last index of the element after the operation).
I have written the above code in VS2015 and when I run the tests, the code has passed without any exception or failure. That means the PexTestCaseGenerator has generated a test method correctly but not all the conditions are satisfied.
The expected assertion "If the return value is false, index of x before is more than or equal to after" is never reached. So, my observation about the expected failure to be generated is incorrect.
Could you please help me spot where is my test generation incorrect or broken? Because I believe the test should be correct as the documentation says. Or maybe the example I've used in this case might be too complex/advanced?

A: The method signature is incorrect - the ArrayList.IndexOf() and ArrayList.LastIndexOf() methods do not return an index of the element you're searching for if the element doesn't exist in the ArrayList. Instead, they return -1. If the element doesn't exist in the ArrayList, then the IndexOf and LastIndexOf methods should return less than 0 values. Here is correctly formulated Assert condition:
```csharp
if (New_Ret == false)
{
    if (Old_arrListIndexOfX < 0)
    {
        if (New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0)
        {
            throw new PexExpectedException("The index of x is not less than 0 and more than or equal to after the operation, while the return value is false");
        }
    }
}
```
Remember that ArrayList.Contains() method returns true if the value is found in the ArrayList, otherwise, it returns false. From the ArrayList.IndexOf() and ArrayList.LastIndexOf() methods you can't determine that the element was not added, because those methods return negative value (-1) if the value wasn't found. You have to use the ArrayList.Contains() method as a marker of the state of the ArrayList before and after calling the property method. PexFuzz testing is better in this situation as it allows to cover the "false-positive" cases, when your methods return false, but the value actually exists in the ArrayList.

From the description it seems like a bug in PexTestCaseGenerator. I'd recommend to log an issue in the Pex tool’s official repository and specifying a test that you've written to demonstrate the issue. They will be pleased to help with a possible solution. Also it'd be beneficial to compare the initial and final state of the ArrayList to identify whether it changed as expected or not. This may help pinpoint the issues better.

Another thing to note is that Pex generates comprehensive and useful tests, but the final decision on the necessity of a particular test depends on the test environment, test coverage settings and the overall state of the generated tests. Depending on the environment and the test coverage settings Pex might generate too many of the tests or miss some, in such case, it is always beneficial to check manually and fine-tune the settings or adjust the logic. 

Here is an improved version of your code with more detailed comments:

```csharp
